  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 245 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nThis wave tracking across the Desert Southwest into the southern \nPlains is almost universally modeled to interact with a quick-moving \nnorthern stream wave from the northern Plains. Most solutions now \nmodel the waves phasing constructively, strengthening the overall \nsystem through the day on Wednesday. Exactly how amplified this wave \ngets is the most uncertain aspect of the flow pattern, according to \nEOF analysis of the ensemble members. That said, there is a fair \namount of confidence that the main surface low will form across the \nGulf Coast and curl northward along the Appalachians. \n\nAll available guidance does show the northern stream wave forcing a \nsecondary area of precipitation across the Midwest further from the \nmain cyclone late Wednesday through Thanksgiving. While guidance is \nnatively showing a wide range of precipitation types across eastern \nMissouri and western Illinois, including the 06Z ECMWF showing a \nserious amount of snow, thermodynamic profiles are consistently \nunfavorable for much snow at all. The main limiting factor is the \nlack of sufficiently-cold air aloft: most guidance features a nose \nof above-freezing wetbulb temperatures aloft, some as warm as 2-3 \ndegrees Celsius. That would melt any chance for snow, but even if a \nfew intrepid flakes can survive this, ground temperatures will still \nbe warm enough to preclude impacts. Any model output showing \nsignificant snowfall amounts in the bi-state region are not taking \nthese factors into account, and are using a 10:1 snow-liquid ratio \nthat will certainly not exist. While I can't rule out a few \nsnowflakes mixed into the rain, the threat for impacts to travel (or \neven accumulations) are low (10-20%) and limited to northeast of the \nSt. Louis metropolitan area. The remainder of the holiday weekend is \nforecast to be tranquil with a gradual warming trend.",
